These scams work through urgency (“pay now or get fined”) and small amounts that lower people’s guard.

2. The bonus attachment (file-based vector plus social engineering). it shows two things working together: a malicious file, and curiosity as the hook. Everyone wants to know their bonus. A file can pretend to be something it isn’t. A classic trick is Bonuses_2026.pdf.exe, which Windows may display as just Bonuses_2026.pdf.

3. Your old computer (unsupported system). If it can no longer receive patches, that’s an unsupported system rather than just vulnerable software. Vulnerable software means patches exist but haven’t been applied. Unsupported means nobody is making patches anymore. And keeping it offline is exactly what a security pro would do. You’re removing it from the attack surface. In Security+ terms, that’s isolation, and it’s a good compensating control.

4. The malicious charging cable (removable device). This is real. There are cables that look identical to normal ones but hide a tiny chip. They can pretend to be a keyboard and type commands, or record keystrokes. That’s why you should never use a charger or cable you didn’t buy yourself, and why public USB charging stations are risky.

5. Browser-based apps (agentless). You’re trusting the provider to keep it secure, because there’s nothing on your machine for you to patch.

6. Target and the HVAC company (supply chain). This is the textbook example. What makes it even better is that attackers phished the HVAC company first, stole its login to Target’s vendor portal, and then moved from there into the payment systems. It’s two vectors chained together: phishing led to a supply chain attack. Real attacks often work like this.

