Overview: Misconfiguration Vulnerabilities 2.3  

The big idea: The software itself might be fine. The problem is how someone set it up. Messer describes these as the vulnerabilities we create ourselves. Think of it like buying a top-quality deadlock and leaving the key in it.

Misconfigurations are very common because systems ship with settings designed to be easy, not secure, and busy staff don’t always go back and tighten them.

The five types to know:

1. Open permissions
Files or cloud storage left open so anyone on the internet can read them. A classic example is a company’s cloud storage “bucket” accidentally set to public, exposing millions of customer records. Big companies have done this many times. It’s like leaving your filing cabinet on the footpath.

2. Unsecured admin accounts
The all-powerful accounts (Administrator in Windows, root in Linux) with weak passwords, or used for everyday work. If an attacker gets one, they own the system. Good practice is to use a normal account day to day and only “step up” to admin when needed. Your ANZ example of forgetting to log off as admin fits perfectly here.

3. Insecure protocols
Old protocols that send everything, including passwords, in plain text. Anyone listening on the network can read them. The insecure versions have secure replacements:

Telnet → SSH
FTP → SFTP
HTTP → HTTPS

It’s like the difference between sending a postcard and a sealed letter. You’ll remember a lot of these from Network+.

4. Default settings
Devices left with factory passwords and settings. A famous real case is the Mirai botnet (2016). It scanned the internet for cameras and routers still using default logins like admin/admin, took over hundreds of thousands of them, and used them to knock major websites offline.

5. Open ports and services
Services left running that nobody uses, or firewall rules that allow far more than needed. Every open port is another door into the house, which links back to the attack surface from 2.2.

CompTIA Security+ 2.3: Misconfiguration Vulnerabilities
Why are misconfigurations so common?
A misconfiguration vulnerability isn’t a flaw in the software itself. It’s a weakness created by how something was set up. It’s like buying a top-quality deadlock and leaving the key in it. These are some of the most common vulnerabilities in the real world, because we create them ourselves.

Why are misconfigurations so common? There are several reasons. Devices ship with default settings designed to be easy to use rather than secure, so they work straight out of the box. Many people don’t realise those defaults are publicly known. Time pressure plays a big part too: if you’ve just spent half an hour getting a new security camera working, changing the default password feels like one more chore. In home and small business environments, security is often an afterthought. People just want things up and running.

Default settings, and what your Wi-Fi name gives away. Every model of modem and camera has default settings and logins that anyone can look up online. When I checked the Wi-Fi networks around my area, I noticed several still using the default network name (SSID), showing the brand and even the exact model number. That tells an attacker exactly what equipment is being used, so they can look up its default admin login and any known vulnerabilities. Many newer routers come with a unique Wi-Fi password on a sticker, but the admin login is often left at the default. This is how the Mirai botnet (2016) worked: it scanned the internet for cameras and routers still using default logins like admin/admin, took over hundreds of thousands of them, and used them to take major websites offline.

Unsecured admin accounts. When I worked in IT support at ANZ, we logged on to users’ computers with our admin accounts to install software or make changes. With people constantly asking for help, it was very easy to walk away and accidentally leave the computer logged in as admin, giving the user full control of the machine. This is why the principle of least privilege matters: use a normal account day to day, and only step up to admin when it’s needed.

Orphaned accounts. At Orica, a big part of my job was opening and closing user accounts, and accounts were closed quickly when someone left. In companies with high staff turnover, there can be a gap where someone has left but their account is still active. These are called orphaned or dormant accounts, and they give a former employee, or anyone who knows the login, continued access. Shared logins make it worse, because they break non-repudiation: if several people use one account, you can’t prove who did what.

Insecure protocols. Older protocols send everything, including passwords, in plain text, like sending a postcard instead of a sealed letter. Telnet should be replaced with SSH, FTP with SFTP, and HTTP with HTTPS. The same goes for Wi-Fi security: older standards like WEP and WPA are easily broken, so networks should use WPA2 or WPA3. Many people are still running old modems on outdated security without realising how exposed they are. An old modem that no longer receives firmware updates is also a separate hardware/end-of-life vulnerability, another part of 2.3.

Open ports and services. Every open port is another door into the house. The risk isn’t a port for a service that’s been fully stopped, because nothing is listening there. The risk is a service left running that nobody uses anymore, or a firewall rule left open after the service has gone. Regularly reviewing what’s running and what the firewall allows helps keep the attack surface small.

