Overview: Mobile Device Vulnerabilities 2.3

The big idea: Phones come with built-in guard rails. The official app store checks apps before you can install them, and the operating system keeps each app in its own “room” so it can’t interfere with other apps or the system. Most mobile vulnerabilities come from removing those guard rails.

1. Sideloading
Installing an app from outside the official store, for example downloading an app file from a website or getting one sent to you. Nobody has checked it for malware. It’s like buying medicine from a stranger’s car boot instead of a pharmacy. It might be fine, but there’s no quality control. Android allows sideloading more easily. Apple has traditionally blocked it, though rules are loosening in some countries.

2. Jailbreaking (iPhone) and rooting (Android)
Deliberately breaking the phone’s built-in restrictions to get full control. This lets people install anything and change system settings. But it also removes the walls that keep malware contained, so a bad app could reach everything. It’s like taking out every internal door and lock in your house because they’re inconvenient. Jailbroken phones may also stop receiving security updates.

3. Why businesses care
If a jailbroken or rooted phone has work email, files or company apps on it, the risk spreads to the whole organisation. That’s why workplaces use MDM (Mobile Device Management). It can detect jailbroken or rooted phones, block them from company data, enforce passcodes, and even wipe a lost phone remotely.


